.TH ESDM\-SERVER\-SIGNAL\-HELPER 8 "2026-08-05" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
esdm\-server\-signal\-helper \- notify the ESDM server about suspend and resume
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B esdm\-server\-signal\-helper
.B \-\-suspend \-\-pid
.I PIDFILE
.br
.B esdm\-server\-signal\-helper
.B \-\-resume
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B esdm\-server\-signal\-helper
tells a running
.BR esdm\-server (8)
that the system is about to be suspended, and that it has resumed.
.PP
On
.BR \-\-suspend ,
the helper reads the PID of the daemon from its PID file and sends it
.BR SIGUSR1 .
It only does so while the PID file is locked by the process it names, as the
daemon keeps it locked for as long as it runs; a stale PID file left behind by
a daemon that is gone is refused, as its PID may by now belong to an unrelated
process.
The daemon then marks itself as suspended in the status shared memory segment,
so that consumers waiting on it do not block on a daemon that is about to stop
running.
.PP
On
.BR \-\-resume ,
the helper connects to the privileged RPC interface and asks the daemon to
reseed all of its DRNGs, as
.B RNDRESEEDCRNG
does for the kernel's random number generator: the seeds they hold are from
before the suspend, and the reseed interval alone would keep them in service
for up to its whole length afterwards. The reseed worker of the daemon reseeds
them right away, and a DRNG asked for data before it got there is reseeded
first. Only root may ask for this, which is what the shipped resume unit runs
as; for anybody else the helper fails. No PID file is needed for it.
.PP
The reseed interval of the daemon counts the time the system was suspended as
well, so a DRNG whose interval elapsed during a long suspend is reseeded at its
next use even without the helper.
.PP
Both operations are wired to the sleep targets by the shipped
.BR esdm\-server\-suspend.service " and " esdm\-server\-resume.service
systemd units, so the helper rarely needs to be invoked by hand.
.SH OPTIONS
Exactly one of
.BR \-\-suspend " and " \-\-resume
has to be given.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-suspend
Signal the impending suspend.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-resume
Signal the completed resume, which has the daemon reseed its DRNGs. Requires
root.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pid " \fIPIDFILE\fR"
Read the PID of the daemon from
.IR PIDFILE .
Required by
.BR \-\-suspend ,
which does not guess a PID file and fails without one; not used by
.BR \-\-resume .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit with a non\-zero status.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 when the requested notification was delivered, and a non\-zero status
otherwise \- including when no mode, both modes or an unknown option or
operand was given.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/esdm\-server.pid
PID file written by
.BR esdm\-server (8)
when started with
.BR \-\-pid ,
and the file the shipped suspend unit passes to this helper.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR esdm\-server (8),
.BR esdm\-tool (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Stephan Mueller <smueller@chronox.de>
